WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:13.235
Hi, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:13.595 --> 00:00:14.395
It's me, Jake!

3
00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:18.956
This weekend my family and I are going to New York City to visit my cousin.

4
00:00:19.876 --> 00:00:23.137
He goes to college there, and guess what—I just found out that Mr.

5
00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:24.918
Fay went to the same college!

6
00:00:25.898 --> 00:00:30.179
Our teacher loved living in New York, and he said he especially loved going to Central Park.

7
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:31.660
Then Mr.

8
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.487
Fay's face lit up, and he said that Central Park would make a great subject for a landmarks

9
00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:42.012
lesson! Located in the middle of Manhattan, Central Park is one of the most famous parks in the

10
00:00:42.052 --> 00:00:52.601
world. This 3.4-square kilometer park contains rocky hills, lakes, ponds, open meadows, dense woodlands—things you'd never expect to

11
00:00:52.641 --> 00:00:55.403
find in a bustling city with millions of residents!

12
00:00:56.604 --> 00:01:00.868
And amazingly, almost all of the landscape was created artificially.

13
00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:06.047
The idea for a large public park in New York started in the 1840s.

14
00:01:07.048 --> 00:01:11.911
The city's population was growing fast, and a lot of rich people wanted a quiet place where they

15
00:01:11.931 --> 00:01:15.193
could escape from the crowds and the noise, and just enjoy nature.

16
00:01:16.134 --> 00:01:20.757
Park planners selected a large area of swampland and rocky bluffs in the middle of the city.

17
00:01:21.658 --> 00:01:28.903
About 1,600 poor immigrants and freed slaves lived there in shantytowns, mostly farming and raising livestock, until about

18
00:01:28.923 --> 00:01:35.523
1853. That was when the city's government was authorized to take over the land, and they began forcing

19
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:36.343
everyone off it.

20
00:01:37.184 --> 00:01:42.527
Park commissioners held a landscape design contest and received 33 submissions.

21
00:01:42.587 --> 00:01:49.772
In 1858 judges chose a plan by a landscape designer, Frederick Law Olmsted, and Calvert Vaux, an architect.

22
00:01:51.292 --> 00:01:56.716
Inspired by parks in England, the plan harmoniously blended man-made elements with natural elements.

23
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.862
Over twenty thousand workers spent about three years creating the park's landscape.

24
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:07.266
They used 150 metric tons of gunpowder to blast away giant boulders.

25
00:02:08.326 --> 00:02:14.850
They also filled in swampy areas, created seven bodies of water, and planted nearly three hundred thousand trees.

26
00:02:15.931 --> 00:02:21.374
When the park was finished, it contained a wide, tree-lined avenue and an enormous terrace and fountain.

27
00:02:22.394 --> 00:02:27.562
Separate paths for walking, horseback riding, and carriage driving were laid out around the park.

28
00:02:28.622 --> 00:02:34.306
Thirty-six unique bridges allowed these paths to cross over and under each other, so that everyone could enjoy

29
00:02:34.346 --> 00:02:36.968
the natural surroundings without bumping into each other.

30
00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:43.812
At first Central Park was used mainly by rich people, who rode their carriages along the park's roads.

31
00:02:44.833 --> 00:02:48.815
Most of the city's poorer residents lived too far from the park to visit easily.

32
00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:55.056
Plus, park commissioners created lots of rules that made it difficult for working class people to use the

33
00:02:55.096 --> 00:03:00.501
space. People weren't allowed to play any sports on the lawns, and group picnics weren't permitted.

34
00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:06.846
Drivers of commercial wagons couldn't take them into the park, and events such as concerts were never held

35
00:03:06.906 --> 00:03:11.110
on Sundays, the one day of the week when working class people didn't have to work.

36
00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.713
Over time these rules were eliminated, as more and more people protested them.

37
00:03:17.195 --> 00:03:21.649
By the end of the nineteenth century, people of all social classes were visiting the park to picnic,

38
00:03:21.969 --> 00:03:24.470
play ball games, and attend Sunday concerts.

39
00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.831
Today Central Park is much more than just a place to enjoy nature.

40
00:03:29.791 --> 00:03:34.773
It contains a large number of sculptures, memorials, and fountains scattered throughout the area.

41
00:03:35.713 --> 00:03:45.596
Each year thirty-eight million people visit for activities like running, biking, swimming, bird watching, rock climbing, concerts—even roller-skating

42
00:03:45.656 --> 00:03:46.576
dance parties!

43
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:55.906
The park has 21 playgrounds, a zoo, a carousel, an ice-skating rink, boat rentals, restaurants, a puppet theater,

44
00:03:56.206 --> 00:03:59.809
and wide lawns where visitors can play Frisbee or just lie in the sun.

45
00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:08.174
An enormous reservoir, which holds over 3.5 million cubic meters of water, takes up about an eighth of

46
00:04:08.215 --> 00:04:08.935
the parkland.

47
00:04:09.936 --> 00:04:15.019
Even though it no longer supplies New York City with drinking water, this reservoir is still an important

48
00:04:15.059 --> 00:04:15.740
part of the city.

49
00:04:16.692 --> 00:04:21.575
Mr. Fay said it's a wonderful place to watch the sun set, and if you're feeling energetic, you

50
00:04:21.595 --> 00:04:25.097
can go running on the 2.5-kilometer track that loops around it.

51
00:04:26.017 --> 00:04:30.700
The track is a famous spot in New York City; it's even been featured in books, TV shows,

52
00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:31.500
and movies.

53
00:04:32.561 --> 00:04:37.603
In the summer the park's open-air Delacorte Theater is home to Shakespeare in the Park, a program that

54
00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:41.585
features performances of Shakespeare's works as well as other plays and musicals.

55
00:04:43.246 --> 00:04:48.192
Tickets are free, but if you want one, you'd better get there early—Shakespeare in the Park is very

56
00:04:48.272 --> 00:04:54.634
popular! Also located in the park is the Metropolitan Museum of Art, one of the world's most well-known

57
00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:55.354
art museums.

58
00:04:56.355 --> 00:04:59.315
I love to draw, so I was really excited to find that out!

59
00:05:00.156 --> 00:05:04.017
Mr. Fay told us that Central Park's oldest structure is right behind the museum.

60
00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:12.299
It's a tall Egyptian obelisk called Cleopatra's Needle, and it was built in—get this—1450 BC!

61
00:05:13.319 --> 00:05:14.459
Egypt gave it to the U.S.

62
00:05:14.519 --> 00:05:20.555
as a gift in 1877, and four years later, the obelisk was carefully erected in Central Park.

63
00:05:21.596 --> 00:05:24.578
Most kids' favorite spot in the park is the Central Park Zoo.

64
00:05:25.578 --> 00:05:32.763
It's open all year and is home to over 130 different species, including polar bears, snow monkeys, penguins,

65
00:05:33.084 --> 00:05:34.064
and leafcutter ants.

66
00:05:35.705 --> 00:05:40.248
The zoo has a special children's section where little kids can feed goats and other farm animals.

67
00:05:41.249 --> 00:05:44.972
A very popular attraction at the zoo is feeding time at the sea lion pool!

68
00:05:45.984 --> 00:05:49.548
Despite all these fun things to do and see in Central Park, Mr.

69
00:05:49.608 --> 00:05:53.913
Fay said he likes nothing better than buying a soft pretzel from a street vendor, sitting on a

70
00:05:53.933 --> 00:05:56.155
park bench, and just watching people go by.

71
00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:59.158
Well, I should start packing for my trip.

72
00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:03.843
I'm sure there's a lot my family and I can do in New York City, but there's just

73
00:06:04.083 --> 00:06:06.346
one place I want to go—Central Park!

74
00:06:07.347 --> 00:06:08.108
See you next week!
